The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
if (f.binaryValue() == null) {
    return null;
}
return indexedToReadable(f.binaryValue().utf8ToString());
```
Reason:
The logic in the `toExternal` method incorrectly returns `null` when `binaryValue()` is `null`, without checking if `stringValue()` is also `null`. This leads to a situation where the method returns "false" for all documents if both values are absent, failing to provide a valid string representation when only one of the values is present.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
if (null != f.binaryValue()) {
    return indexedToReadable(f.binaryValue().utf8ToString());
}
if (null != f.stringValue()) {
    return indexedToReadable(f.stringValue());
}
return null;
```
Reason:
The fix introduces checks for both `binaryValue()` and `stringValue()`, ensuring that if one is present, its valid string representation is returned. This prevents the method from erroneously returning "false" when both values are `null`, thus correcting the behavior when querying documents.

